        "https://www.bradford.ac.uk/news/archive/2020/high-level-of-interest-in-university-of-bradfords-former-business-school-hq-mansion-house.php": {
          "url": "https://www.bradford.ac.uk/news/archive/2020/high-level-of-interest-in-university-of-bradfords-former-business-school-hq-mansion-house.php",
          "status": 200,
          "finalUrl": "https://www.bradford.ac.uk/news/archive/2020/high-level-of-interest-in-university-of-bradfords-former-business-school-hq-mansion-house.php",
          "intro": "High level of interest in University of Bradford’s former business school HQ mansion house Published: Wednesday 16 December 2020 Places filling up for final viewing day Places are already filling up for the final viewing day for the sale of one of Bradford’s historic mansion houses, the Grade II-listed Heaton Mount/Emm Lane complex. The 14-acre site, described as “an exquisite example of the Italian architectural style” is being marketed on a freehold basis subject to informal tender. Boasting an internal area of 121,000sq ft (11,240sq m) it has potential for a variety of uses, subject to necessary consents. Two viewing dates have already taken place, with a third and final date scheduled for January 12. To date, more than 50 parties have registered an interest in the site, which boasts three Grade II-listed buildings, the largest of which, Heaton Mount, was built for businessman and treasurer of the Bradford Liberal Electoral Association for several years by Robert Kell in 1886. Dai Powell, from marketing agents Avison Young, said: “Thus far we have been pleased with the level of interest shown in what is a unique site in Bradford, boasting a range of facilities and opportunities in terms of future use.” The University is selling the estate following the successful relocation of the Faculty of Management and Law and consolidation of other educational provisions onto City Campus. Although bids to purchase parts of the property may be considered, it is the University’s strong ",
          "campusSnippets": [
            "s historic mansion houses, the Grade II-listed Heaton Mount/Emm Lane complex. The 14-acre site, described as “an exquisite example of the Italian architectural style” is being marketed on a freehold basis subject to informal tender. Boasting an int",
            "and consolidation of other educational provisions onto City Campus. Although bids to purchase parts of the property may be considered, it is the University’s strong preference to sell in a single transaction. Informal tenders are invited by 1pm o",
            "tate comprises nine buildings, including the GradeII-listed Emm Lane Building and adjoining Sir Titus Salt Building, 44,600sq ft (4,144sq m), which includes a lecture theatre, restaurant and atrium. Heaton Mount, 24,200sq ft (2,249sq m), also Grade"
          ]
        },
